  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 243 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge and hot air mass will remain over the \nregion through much of the week. 850mb temperatures in the\nlower 20C will be in place resulting in highs in the middle to\nupper 90s and lows in the 70s the rest of the week and\npossibly into the start of the weekend. The ensemble model \nmembers show an upper level trough and a front approaching the \narea late this weekend which would result in slightly cooler \ntemperatures across the area. The Heat Advisory is now in effect\nfor the entire area on Tuesday as highs in the middle to upper\n90s with heat index values around 100 to 105 degrees are\nexpected. \n\nInstability and the cap will weaken with the heating of the day\neach day this week and could result in isolated pulse type\nstorms during the afternoon/evening hours Tuesday through the\nFriday. A weak front will push south into the northern portions\nof the area later Tuesday into Tuesday night. The front will \nbring scattered storm chances to the area, mainly north of I-44\nTuesday evening and night. With the boundary over the area and \nuncapped MUCAPE in place all night, storms could occur off and \non through much of the night north of the front into early \nWednesday morning. The low level flow and storm mode will be \nsouth to north across the area, so its possible that if storms \ndevelop and create an outflow it could pushes the \nactivity/effected front back to the north of the area Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning. There will be the potential for a \nfew strong to severe storms with damaging winds the main risk \nmainly north of Highway 54 on Tuesday into Tuesday night. The \nfront will push back to the north on Wednesday with the better \nwidespread rain chances remaining north of the area through the \nrest of the week.",
